WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.480
- What's the most important thing,

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:02.670
Emma, I'm gonna ask you first,

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.400
what's the most important
lesson, you think,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.960
about motherhood that you
learned from your mother?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.273
- Ooh!
- Oh gosh.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.900
I remember when both my kids were young,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.237
I remember Mom specifically
saying to me, you know,

00:00:17.237 --> 00:00:20.897
"Just be patient,
because just when you get

00:00:20.897 --> 00:00:22.607
"to that moment where you think,

00:00:22.607 --> 00:00:24.307
"I am not gonna be able to survive

00:00:24.307 --> 00:00:26.407
"another sleepless night,
or I'm not gonna be able

00:00:26.407 --> 00:00:30.187
"to survive this particular
developmental stage,

00:00:30.187 --> 00:00:32.487
"just when you think you
can't go another day,

00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:34.930
"it will change, and move
on to the next thing."

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.930
And it always did.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.318
She was so right about
that, and always, you know--

00:00:39.318 --> 00:00:40.151
- [Julie] It is true.
- Just thought,

00:00:40.151 --> 00:00:42.073
"Oh my God, I'll never
survive this moment."

00:00:42.073 --> 00:00:42.906
(laughing loudly)

00:00:42.906 --> 00:00:45.030
They were out of it, and on
to the next thing, you know.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.480
- Well, Julie, we learn
a lot from our kids, too.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.160
I know I do, even from my
four and five year old.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.090
- Always.
- Yeah!

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.980
- And they fill in all
the gaps that didn't

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.420
make sense when you
were a kid, don't they?

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.670
- Wow, that's a really
great way to put it,

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.140
I've never thought of it that way.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.390
Well, what's a lesson that you feel

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.980
like you've learned from Emma?

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.470
- So many things.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.023
I mean, more love than you can imagine.

00:01:07.023 --> 00:01:10.800
I mean, I love my kids,
and I didn't particularly

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.070
when I was pregnant and things.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.150
I didn't think that I
loved kids that much.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.960
It's a great gift, in every way, yeah.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.060
- I feel like I'm the same way.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.000
I was, my Mom is still surprised

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.110
that I married with children.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:25.060
- Kelly, you have to
mention the other three,

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.390
and what their names are, please.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.590
- Oh, yes, oh, I will totally do that,

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:30.540
I have it right here.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.603
And you know what, that's
actually important, 'cause you--

00:01:31.603 --> 00:01:33.720
- What do you mean, I have it right here?

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.320
You don't remember their--

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.510
- I, wait, my kids or yours?

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.030
- You have their names
written down or something.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.370
- Oh no, your kids, Kelly, your kids!

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:43.760
- Oh, I thought you were
talking about yours,

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:44.600
I was like, oh my gosh!

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.070
- No, no no no, I'm asking you.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.360
- Oh no, I was like, I was
like, I was about to tell you,

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.020
it's actually really important for me

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.270
that every time I do an interview,

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.360
everybody always says, "Oh,
you have two children."

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.740
And I'm like, "No, we have four."

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.220
I've been in their lives for eight years,

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.790
and we're blended, so we,
Savannah's our 18 year old,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.430
she's actually an equestrian,
she does eventing,

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.320
and she's actually number
one in her division,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.786
in the country, so she's amazing.

00:02:06.786 --> 00:02:07.619
- Wow.

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:08.950
- And then we have our 13 year old, Seth,

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.040
he's awesome, he's our little gamer,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.980
and he like does 3D stings, and he,

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.910
he just creates, he's
a really creative kid.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.510
- Oh wow.
- And he's actually been

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.585
my teleprompter guy for
this whole pandemic,

00:02:20.585 --> 00:02:21.740
while I've been--
- There you are,

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.120
you see, like Sam is here, helping me.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.130
- You're working Sam,
and I'm working Seth.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.087
Both Biblical names, as well (laughs).

00:02:29.358 --> 00:02:30.191
- And the other one?

00:02:30.191 --> 00:02:32.340
- River and Remington,
those are the tiny ones,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.390
and they're driving me nuts,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.040
so I could use that patience
talk right now (laughs).

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:38.890
But I was gonna say, I'm like you, though,

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.760
I didn't want kids, I didn't
think I'd ever have kids,

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.780
and then once you do, you're right, Julie,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.110
like once you do, it teaches
you an amazing amount

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:48.733
of selflessness.
- Yes.

00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:51.200
- You know, I didn't realize
how selfish I was, and I--

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.551
- The wellspring of love that comes up,

00:02:53.551 --> 00:02:57.170
and out of you, I mean,
I now know why people say

00:03:00.095 --> 00:03:01.107
I get in front of--

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:02.870
- Like a train, you're right.
- A machine gun,

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.150
if it saved my kids, and I would.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.070
- I would do anything for my kids, yeah.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:08.540
No, I am definitely that.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:09.870
And we do learn from them every day,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.800
it's funny, you learn, I
think, more about yourself,

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.820
and how you act around
them, and you're like,

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:16.620
I could maybe work on that (laughs).

